MEDUSA_EMAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Medusa Cloud injects these when the app runs there. The Cloud transport needs
# an API key, an endpoint, and one of the two handles before it reports itself
# available, so setting them is how to exercise that transport locally: point
# the endpoint at something that records requests and answers 200 with
# {"id":"..."} — integration-tests/helpers/capture-server.ts is the one the
# test suite uses. Uncomment and fill all three.
#
# Commented out rather than left empty, unlike the keys above. An empty
# assignment is still a set variable, and Medusa treats any MEDUSA_CLOUD_*
# value that is not `undefined` as opting in: it then builds a `cloud` block
# into projectConfig and merges it into the notification, payment, search and
# auth module options. Nothing registers, because empty strings are falsy, but
# four core modules get configuration they should never have seen.
#
# Medusa reads these while evaluating `defineConfig` in medusa-config.ts, so
# they have to be in the environment before the app boots. Exporting them into
# a running process is too late, and the transport stays unavailable with no
# indication why.
#
# With none of them set the transport reports itself unavailable and the admin
# API answers 409, which is what every installation not on Cloud should see.
# MEDUSA_CLOUD_API_KEY=
# MEDUSA_CLOUD_EMAILS_ENDPOINT=
# MEDUSA_CLOUD_ENVIRONMENT_HANDLE=
# Either handle satisfies the check; the sandbox one is the alternative.
# MEDUSA_CLOUD_SANDBOX_HANDLE=